# limpiador — Agent Development Rules (.clauderules)

These rules govern how Claude Code works on this repository. They exist to
protect token budget, production code integrity, and architectural coherence
across a focused five-day build. Read them before each working session.

# 1. NO "BRUTE FORCE" DEBUGGING
- If a fix fails twice, STOP. Do not try a third time blindly.
- Ask the user for guidance or propose a new, explicitly stated hypothesis.
- Never loop endlessly consuming tokens. The agent we are BUILDING has a
  call-count kill-switch for exactly this reason; the same discipline applies to
  the engineer building it.

# 2. PRODUCTION CODE SANCTITY
- NEVER modify production code (`src/limpiador/`) to bypass a test failure
  (e.g., "if MOCK_MODE return True" sprinkled into the loop or a tool).
- If a test fails, fix the bug or fix the test, not reality.
- Mocks belong in `tests/`. The one mock model adapter is injected into the
  agent core through the real LLM interface — it is never branched on inside
  `src/limpiador/`. Production code must not know whether it is talking to a real
  or a mock model.

# 3. ROOT CAUSE FIRST
- When seeing an error (e.g., `AttributeError`, a malformed tool result),
  analyze the STACK TRACE first.
- Do not guess. Read the code where the error originates before proposing a fix.
- For agent-behavior failures, read the trace (`observability/tracing.py`
  output) before reading code — the trace tells you which tool call went wrong.

# 4. TOKEN ECONOMY
- Do not re-read the entire codebase for every small task.
- If a task needs > 5 files or > 1000 lines of context total:
  - Explain why, and ask the user for guidance first.
- Be specific with `@file` references when requesting context. Prefer
  `@src/limpiador/tools/registry.py` over "the tools".
- This mirrors the product itself: limpiador summarizes-and-evicts rather than
  re-reading. Work the same way on the repo.

# 5. ARCHITECTURAL INTEGRITY
- Respect the layers (see ARCHITECTURE.md §3). Dependencies point downward only.
- NEVER import `tests/` or `evals/` into `src/limpiador/`.
- All provider-specific code stays in `agent/llm.py`. Do not let OpenAI types
  leak into the loop, the tools, or the schemas.
- Every tool consumes and emits typed objects from `schemas.py`. Do not pass
  free text between tools, and do not relabel a plain function call as a
  subagent — the reviewer subagent must keep its isolated context and scoped
  registry.
- Keep the registry coherent: a new tool is registered and discovered via
  `search_tools`. Do not add conditional dispatch branches to the loop.

# 6. TEST-DRIVEN FIXES & TEST PYRAMID
- MANDATORY TDD WORKFLOW for all bug fixes and features:
  1. WRITE TESTS FIRST — unit tests (tool logic, registry, context) + eval cases
     (real-model reasoning on a seeded fixture) where behavior is the thing
     under test.
  2. RUN TESTS — verify they all FAIL (Red Phase). If any pass, the test is wrong.
  3. FIX THE CODE — make the minimal change to pass the tests.
  4. RUN TESTS AGAIN — verify they all PASS (Green Phase).
  5. RUN FULL REGRESSION — `make test` to catch regressions.
  6. Only then commit.
- When fixing a bug, read the failing test first. Understand the expected
  behavior. Only then read the production code.
- NEVER skip writing tests. NEVER fix code without a failing test first.
- Group related work: write ALL tests for a batch, then fix ALL, then verify ALL.

## Test Pyramid Strategy
Every fix AND new feature includes tests at the right layers.

### Layer 1: Unit Tests (tests/unit/) — FAST, mock model, no network
- Extract core logic into pure functions and test them directly.
- Mock external dependencies (OpenAI, GitHub API, subprocess).
- Cover edge cases exhaustively: empty, null, boundary, malformed tool input.
- Target: dozens of tests, < 1s total runtime.

### Layer 2: Mock Integration (tests/integration/*_mock.py) — mock model, real temp repos
- Drive the full agent loop with the deterministic mock LLM over scripted
  tool-call sequences; assert the loop reaches the expected end state.
- Validate registry loading, context compaction, retry/backoff, and that the
  reviewer subagent runs isolated with its scope enforced.
- Use the temp-git-repo and loaded-registry fixtures from `conftest.py`.
- Target: a handful per feature, seconds total.

### Layer 3: E2E (tests/integration/*_e2e.py) — REAL OpenAI + REAL git/github
- Invoke limpiador the way a user does — through the CLI, not by calling the
  loop in-process. The PLUMBING is under test: arg parsing, agent boot, auth,
  a real commit landing, a real PR opening, clean exit.
- Runs ONLY against the THROWAWAY sandbox repo (`LIMPIADOR_SANDBOX_REPO`).
  NEVER point E2E at a real repo.
- Skip gracefully (`pytest.skip`) when `OPENAI_API_KEY`, `GITHUB_TOKEN`, or
  `LIMPIADOR_SANDBOX_REPO` is unset, so CI stays green.
- Use RELAXED assertions (the model is non-deterministic).
- Keep count LOW (slow, costs credits): 2-4 tests. Run via `make test-e2e`,
  never in the default `make test`.

### Reproduction Tests (tests/reproduce/) — REAL model
- One test per reported behavior. Starts 🔴 FAILING (TDD red), becomes
  🟢 PASSING after the fix. Uses the real model to match actual behavior.

### Eval Cases (evals/cases/) — REAL OpenAI, seeded fixtures — DISTINCT FROM E2E
- Eval cases test the AGENT'S REASONING; E2E tests the PIPELINE. Same run mode,
  different concern, different directory. Do NOT merge them.
- Run the agent against a committed fixture repo with a KNOWN seeded defect.
- Assert on BOTH outcome (goal achieved) and trace (reasoned correctly, right
  tool order, under the call ceiling).
- Keep ground-truth checks binary even where prose is non-deterministic (the
  test now passes; the symbol is renamed everywhere; the planted regression was
  flagged).
- Keep the count LOW (slow, costs credits). Run via `make eval`.

### Rules
- Do NOT write only E2E or eval cases. Always start with unit tests.
- Do NOT skip the mock-integration layer — it catches loop/format/routing bugs
  cheaply on the mock model.
- Every new tool gets unit coverage before it is wired into the registry.
- At least one eval case must carry a planted red herring (an innocent
  recently-changed file beside the truly-broken one).
- E2E and evals run ONLY in REAL mode. Mechanics run ONLY in MOCK mode. Using
  the wrong mode wastes either credits or confidence.

# 7. RUN MODES (CRITICAL)
limpiador is a CLI, not a server. There are no ports. Instead there are two
distinct RUN MODES, selected by env var / Makefile target. ALWAYS use the
correct one.

## MOCK MODE
- **Config:** `LIMPIADOR_LLM=mock`
- **Behaviour:** deterministic mock LLM adapter, temp git fixtures, no network.
- **Purpose:** fast, free, deterministic development and the entire test suite.
- **Start:** `make dev-mock` / `make test`
- **Use for:** unit tests, integration tests, loop mechanics, CI, building any
  feature before spending a real credit.

## REAL MODE
- **Config:** `LIMPIADOR_LLM=openai` (requires `OPENAI_API_KEY`)
- **Behaviour:** real OpenAI model, real git repositories, real GitHub API.
- **Purpose:** exercising the model's actual reasoning.
- **Start:** `make eval` / `make run REPO=... TASK="..."`
- **Use for:** the eval harness, reproducing a specific reported behavior, the
  demo recording.

## CRITICAL: which mode answers which question
- Reasoning/behavior questions ("did it investigate well", "did it catch the
  regression") can ONLY be validated in REAL mode. Mock mode does not exercise
  model judgment and will not replicate it.
- Mechanics questions (routing, dispatch, format, compaction, retry) MUST be
  validated in MOCK mode because they are deterministic and free.
- Using the wrong mode wastes either credits or confidence. Pick deliberately.

# 8. ARCHITECTURAL DEBT TRACKER
Track known limitations that need data or time before investing in a fix.
Tag them in the trace so frequency can be measured.

## ARCH_DEBT_001: Tool search ranking is keyword-based, not semantic
- **Location:** `src/limpiador/tools/registry.py` → `search` / `_rank`
- **Problem:** `search_tools` ranks candidate tools by keyword overlap on tool
  name and description. A query phrased differently from the tool's wording
  (e.g. "where is this function used" vs a `find_references` description that
  says "locate call sites") may rank the right tool low and cause the model to
  load the wrong tool or call `search_tools` again, burning a turn.
- **Current mitigation:** descriptions are written with synonym-rich phrasing to
  widen keyword overlap; covers the common phrasings. Instrumented with a trace
  tag when the model re-searches within two turns.
- **Trace tag:** `[REGISTRY RESEARCH_RETRY]` — count occurrences in run traces.
- **Decision trigger:** if it fires on more than ~15% of runs, invest in the fix.
- **Proper fix:** embed tool descriptions once at startup and rank `search_tools`
  by cosine similarity instead of keyword overlap. Local embeddings, computed
  once, no per-turn model cost.
- **Effort estimate:** Small–Medium — add an embedding step at registry init and
  swap the ranking function; the interface is unchanged.
- **Added:** v0.1

## ARCH_DEBT_002: Context compaction summary is lossy on wide refactors
- **Location:** `src/limpiador/agent/context.py` → eviction / summarize
- **Problem:** when a task touches many files, summarizing-and-evicting early
  reads can drop a detail the agent needs much later (e.g. an import edge noted
  on call 4 that matters on call 22), forcing a re-read.
- **Current mitigation:** symbol-level facts (definitions, references, imports)
  are kept in durable state and never evicted; only raw payloads are dropped.
- **Trace tag:** `[CONTEXT REREAD]` — fires when a file is read twice in a run.
- **Decision trigger:** if re-reads exceed ~2 per long-horizon run on average,
  revisit what counts as durable state.
- **Proper fix:** promote import/dependency edges into durable state at read
  time so they survive eviction without keeping the raw file.
- **Effort estimate:** Small — extend the durable-state schema and the read-time
  extraction.
- **Added:** v0.1
